<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mutara III Rudahigwa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mutara III Rudahigwa was mwami (king) of Rwanda from 1931 to 1959, installed by the Belgian administration of Ruanda-Urundi in place of his father. He converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and in 1946 dedicated the country to Christ the King. His sudden death in 1959 was followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> revolution, which led to the abolition of the monarchy in 1961.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mutara-iii-rudahigwa.jpg" alt="Mutara III Rudahigwa" width="180" height="135" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rwanda Library · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tutsi_king_Mutara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rudahigwa was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyanza-rwanda">Nyanza</a>, the royal capital, to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuhi-v-musinga">Yuhi V Musinga</a> and Queen Kankazi, the first of his eleven wives, and belonged to the Tutsi Abanyiginya clan. He began his education in 1919 at the Colonial School for Chiefs' Sons in Nyanza, became his father's secretary in 1924 and in January 1929 was made a chief over a province. From 1929 Bishop Léon Classe instructed him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and the Belgians groomed him to replace his father.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He took the throne on 16 November 1931, four days after the Belgians deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuhi-v-musinga">Yuhi V Musinga</a> for alleged contact with German agents. In 1943 he became the first Rwandan king to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, baptised Charles Léon Pierre, and his conversion led a wave of baptisms. The famine years of 1941 to 1945 fell in his reign, among them the Ruzagayura famine of 1943–1944, in which 200,000 of a population of around two million died.</p><p>In 1935 the Belgian administration issued identity cards fixing the categories Tutsi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> and Twa. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> a Hutu emancipation movement grew, and although he abolished the ubuhake system of indentured service in 1954, this had little practical effect. In 1956 he demanded independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu-manifesto">Bahutu Manifesto</a> of 1957 denounced Tutsi rule, and Hutu parties formed under Gregoire Kayibanda and Joseph Gitera.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 25 July 1959, the day after arriving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujumbura">Usumbura</a> for a meeting with Belgian officials, he died at the colonial hospital after visiting his Belgian doctor. The Belgians gave conflicting explanations, a cerebral haemorrhage or a reaction to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penicillin">penicillin</a>, and no autopsy was done after the Queen Mother objected. Rumours that the Belgians had killed him spread, and Rwandans stoned Europeans' cars. His half-brother Jean-Baptiste Ndahindurwa succeeded him as Kigeli V. His widow, Rosalie Gicanda, was killed in the 1994 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> against the Tutsi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mutara III Rudahigwa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Belgium deposed Musinga in 1931 for resisting Christianity and installed his son <b>Mutara III Rudahigwa</b>, who was baptised in 1943 and abolished ubuhake in 1954.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-rwanda">Kingdom of Rwanda · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cyangugu, in what is now <a href="/countries/rwanda">Rwanda</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujumbura">Bujumbura</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/burundi">Burundi</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-2.48,28.9&amp;year=1942" aria-label="Cyangugu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5352 2256 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mutara III Rudahigwa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5802 2526h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5802 2526h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-2.48,28.9&amp;year=1942">What was here in 1942? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
